WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:02.402
You know in the first
Jurassic Park movie,

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:05.338
there was that scene with the
glass of water in the car's cupholder,

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:08.942
with ripples on it to show just how heavy
the approaching T-Rex's footsteps were?

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:13.279
Well, this thing behind me is technically
called the "Large Mobile Shaker",

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.248
but the folks here at the
University of Texas?

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:16.716
They call it
the "T-Rex",

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:20.186
because it's a machine designed
to shake the ground itself.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.788
- We have five shakers.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:24.624
They go from a small one,
that's right behind me.

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.393
And we call that an "urban shaker"

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:29.863
because you can bring it downtown
and not get into trouble with shaking.

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:34.334
And then we have very, very large ones,
Liquidator and T-Rex.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:40.373
Liquidator is a low frequency vertical
shaker where we generate surface waves.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:44.044
Like surface waves from earthquakes,
but ours are linear, typically,

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.812
and earthquakes can be non-linear.

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.616
[loud vibration with descending tone]

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.653
T Rex is a tri-axial shaker.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:57.090
Tri-axial means that the shaker,
only one at a time,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.092
it can shake vertically,

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.694
it can shake horizontally,
and it can go transverse.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:09.436
It can go up to about 60,000 pounds force
vertically, and about half horizontally.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.986
We have been all over the United States.

00:01:26.986 --> 00:01:30.723
We have sensors that we've designed
and we will push in

00:01:30.723 --> 00:01:34.494
because there are pushing rams
on the back of Liquidator and T-Rex,

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:38.765
so that we can install sensors
and then watch the wave go down.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:42.902
Many times we're watching the wave
propagate away from us, but other times

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:46.306
it's very helpful
to watch the wave going down,

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:51.744
and seeing how poorly
the soil might behave due

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:54.981
to large amplitude shaking,
which is what you don't want.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.664
We just came back from Wyoming,

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:16.803
where they're going to
build new power plants,

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:19.739
and the earthquake design is critical.

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:23.810
And we generated a 6000-foot-long wave.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.947
Now we generated a two foot long wave,
too, but going through the range.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.717
So that means we could evaluate

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:34.454
the stiffness profile down to half a mile.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:37.090
With no boreholes!

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.629
That is really a true accomplishment

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:48.134
and a very, very cost effective addition
to the design of the plant.

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:53.439
So this is a sensor.
It's a big magnet with a coil.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.708
The coil is going
through the magnetic field.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:59.245
We were out there in Wyoming
and the darn cows walked by.

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:03.316
Fortunately, they didn't break any cables,
but we had to stop testing

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:04.884
because they made more noise.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.487
They were right out there
and we were half a mile away.

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:26.606
Oh, my goodness.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:31.644
Every time we go someplace,
we'll make sure the level won't damage structures.

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:35.448
Once we were on the UCLA campus,
and maybe didn't watch

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:38.351
what we were doing so much, and people
came running out of the building.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:41.521
And that got us in trouble!

